  "denominator_note": "metrics are aggregated over all VRU observations in the sampled episodes; n_detections is reported per condition",
  "interpretation": {
    "headline_monitor_never_wakes": "Across every severity tested -- up to 8x the shipped magnitude (radar_bias 20 m, radar_clutter 320 spurious returns, extrinsic_drift 32 deg) -- radar health never fell below the 0.6 degraded threshold. It stayed in 0.83-0.99. The first audit showed these faults are undetected at one severity; this shows they are undetected across the whole range, so it is not a threshold-tuning problem.",
    "damage_is_real_and_dose_dependent": {
      "radar_bias": "v_lat MAE 0.893 -> 2.676 m/s and intent AUC 0.871 -> 0.707 as offset goes 0.5 -> 20 m",
      "radar_clutter": "v_lat MAE 1.026 -> 1.799 m/s and position RMSE +1.148 m at n_extra=320",
      "extrinsic_drift": "position RMSE +0.605 m at 32 deg; intent AUC stays high (0.90-0.93)"
    },
    "PERVERSE_radar_clutter_raises_health": "The most important finding here. Radar health INCREASES monotonically with clutter: [(10, 0.9125329644444751), (20, 0.9417914880952382), (40, 0.9666865851866483), (80, 0.9784146724888007), (160, 0.9827911655757112), (320, 0.9933333333333333)]. perception.radar_confidence is documented as a density + cluster-tightness proxy because CARLA exposes no SNR, so injecting spurious returns raises measured density and the sensor scores as HEALTHIER precisely as it becomes less trustworthy. This is a structural defect in the health metric, not a tuning issue, and no threshold on this statistic can fix it.",
    "non_monotonic_caveat": "Some cells are non-monotonic (radar_bias position RMSE peaks at 5 m then falls; small biases marginally improve position). With 24 episodes these wiggles are within noise and only the overall trend should be quoted."
  }
